<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
PrivacyFence autostart Task Scheduler task definition (Phase 3.1, revised
again by Phase 13).

installer/privacyfence.iss's [Code] section extracts this template at
install time, substitutes __EXEC_PATH__ with the real installed
"{app}\{#AliasExeName}" path, and registers it via
`schtasks /create /tn "PrivacyFence" /xml <file> /f`.

Structure follows Microsoft's own official "any group member" logon
trigger example (learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/taskschd/
logon-trigger-example xml) as closely as possible, deliberately: an
earlier, more elaborate version of this file (every Settings element a
real exported task commonly carries, e.g. IdleSettings and
ExecutionTimeLimit) built and installed without a compile error but
the task still never actually appeared afterward, one plausible
explanation being an element this repo could not verify against a
real schema validator locally. Kept to only the elements this task
actually needs changed from their own documented defaults, closest to
the confirmed official example, to minimize that risk.

Three details are copied from that example verbatim rather than
simplified away, because each one is load bearing and a previous
revision of this file dropped all three at once:

  * `version="1.2"` on the root element. The Settings elements below
    (MultipleInstancesPolicy, RestartOnFailure) are task schema 1.2
    constructs; a definition that does not declare the version is
    validated against the 1.0 schema, where they do not exist.
  * `id` on Principal, and the matching `Context` on Actions. The
    schema links an action set to the security context it runs under
    by that id reference. With a Principal that carries no id and an
    Actions element that names no Context, the GroupId below is not
    actually bound to anything that runs.
  * The triggers' StartBoundary. LogonTrigger and TimeTrigger both
    inherit it from the abstract trigger type, where it is the point
    from which the trigger is live at all.

LogonTrigger with no UserId fires for any interactive logon, not just
one specific account, and Principal uses GroupId (the built-in Users
group) rather than UserId so the task runs as whichever user just
logged on, in their own session: the same "whichever account is at the
keyboard" scope the macOS LaunchAgent and the Linux .deb's XDG
autostart already have.
The two battery settings are the schema defaults inverted, and they are
here because both defaults are actively wrong for this task. Task
Scheduler defaults DisallowStartIfOnBatteries and StopIfGoingOnBatteries
to true, which on any laptop means: do not start PrivacyFence at sign in
if the machine is on battery, and stop it if the user unplugs. A privacy
gate that silently is not running when the laptop is unplugged fails
closed in the worst possible sense, since the MCP client simply finds no
daemon. Neither default was ever chosen here; they were inherited, and
were only noticed once a test read the definition back out of Task
Scheduler (schtasks /query /xml) rather than reading this file.
MultipleInstancesPolicy is Parallel, not the schema default IgnoreNew,
deliberately: this task can legitimately have more than one concurrent
instance at once, one per user session, since GroupId, unlike a single
UserId, can trigger for several different logged on users at the same
time. It must stay Parallel even with the TimeTrigger below added:
IgnoreNew would stop a redundant tick's spawn (harmless on its own,
since the single-instance lock already makes it exit immediately) but
would also stop a second user's sign-in from ever getting a daemon,
which is a correctness regression this task cannot trade away for a
smaller one.

Phase 13: RestartOnFailure is not a keep-alive and never was, measured
on a real windows-latest runner rather than assumed: Task Scheduler
logs a killed action as a *successfully completed* task (its own
operational log: "Task Scheduler successfully finished ... action
... with return code 2147942401", 2147942401 being 0x80070001, the
action's own non-zero exit as an HRESULT), so an action that ran and
then died never engages this setting at all; it only ever answers a
task that fails to *run*. It stays here anyway, for the narrower thing
it actually does: a launch failure right at LogonTrigger's own moment
(e.g. a locked file mid-upgrade) gets retried within a minute instead
of waiting for the TimeTrigger's next tick. Count is 3, the same value
Task Scheduler's own UI offers by default, rather than an arbitrarily
large number: the restart count is a bounded schema type, so a large
value is a validation risk for no practical gain, and a daemon that has
failed to launch across three one minute retries is not going to be
rescued by a fourth.

Real crash-restart is the new TimeTrigger, added by Phase 13 once
RestartOnFailure was measured not to provide it: an indefinitely
repeating trigger, live immediately (a StartBoundary in the past, same
convention the LogonTrigger already uses) rather than waiting for a
sign-in, on a PT5M interval. Every tick launches the daemon; if one is
already running, the single-instance lock (privacyfence.daemon_main.
_acquire_instance_lock) makes the new process exit immediately, so this
is normally a no-op cold start rather than a second daemon. PT5M is a
deliberate middle point, not tuned by measurement: shorter buys faster
recovery from a crash at the direct cost of a doomed-to-exit
PyInstaller cold start per signed-in user every interval forever
(battery, on a laptop), and this task has no signal at all for "a user
is actively relying on the daemon right now" to trade against that
cost adaptively. Five minutes is short enough that a user is unlikely
to notice PrivacyFence was down, and long enough that the steady-state
per-tick cost (one process spawn that exits at once) stays well below
what a background Task Scheduler task is expected to cost on a laptop.
The LogonTrigger stays alongside it rather than being replaced: without
it, signing in could leave up to one whole PT5M interval of dead time
before the first tick ever launches the daemon.

The XML declaration deliberately carries no encoding attribute.
schtasks.exe reads this file and hands it to MSXML as a Unicode stream,
so a declaration saying UTF-8 (what installer/privacyfence.iss's
SaveStringToFile actually writes) contradicts the stream the parser is
already on, and MSXML cannot switch encodings mid parse. That is not a
guess: with the registration failure finally visible in the install log,
schtasks said exactly this, and nothing else:

    ERROR: The task XML is malformed.
    (1,40)::ERROR: unable to switch the encoding

Column 40 of line 1 is where `encoding="UTF-8"` sat. With no encoding
declared there is nothing to switch to, and the file parses whichever way
schtasks decides to read it; the content here is pure ASCII, so UTF-8 and
UTF-16 readings agree on every character in it either way. Writing real
UTF-16 bytes and declaring UTF-16, the way Task Scheduler's own exports
do, would also work for today's schtasks, but only for a schtasks that
reads the file exactly that way.

NOTE: this comment intentionally avoids the two-hyphen sequence, which
XML comments may not contain anywhere in their body.
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>Starts PrivacyFence when any user signs in, and keeps it running.</Description>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <StartBoundary>2020-01-01T00:00:00</StartBoundary>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    </LogonTrigger>
    <TimeTrigger>
      <StartBoundary>2020-01-01T00:00:00</StartBoundary>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
      <Repetition>
        <Interval>PT5M</Interval>
      </Repetition>
    </TimeTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <GroupId>Builtin\Users</GroupId>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>Parallel</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Interval>PT1M</Interval>
      <Count>3</Count>
    </RestartOnFailure>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>__EXEC_PATH__</Command>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
